{"step_index":34,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-24T15:51:55Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-24T21:21:55+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-24T21:21:56+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/rajan/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/math_exam_2026/Set_A_Question_Paper.txt`\nTotal Lines: 229\nTotal Bytes: 7888\nShowing lines 200 to 229\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n200: \n201: Q34. If y = (sin⁻¹x)², prove that (1 - x²) d²y/dx² - x dy/dx - 2 = 0.\n202: \n203: Q35. Evaluate: ∫ (x² + 1)/(x² - 5x + 6) dx.\n204: \n205: ================================================================================\n206: SECTION E\n207: (Questions 36 to 38 carry 4 marks each. Case-based questions have sub-parts of 2, 1 and 1 marks.)\n208: ================================================================================\n209: \n210: Q36. Case Study 1 - Relations and Functions\n211: Two friends, Rohan and Simran, are studying relations defined on sets. Consider the set A = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5} and the relation R on A defined by R = {(a, b) : |a - b| is even}.\n212: (i) Show that R is an equivalence relation on A.  (2 marks)\n213: (ii) Write the equivalence class of the element 2.  (1 mark)\n214: (iii) Is (1, 5) ∈ R? Justify your answer.  (1 mark)\n215: \n216: Q37. Case Study 2 - Application of Derivatives\n217: A company's total cost of producing x units of a product is given by C(x) = 0.005x³ - 0.02x² + 30x + 5000 (in ₹).\n218: (i) Find the marginal cost function, MC(x) = dC/dx.  (2 marks)\n219: (ii) Find the marginal cost when x = 3 units.  (1 mark)\n220: (iii) State, with reason, whether the cost is increasing or decreasing at x = 3.  (1 mark)\n221: \n222: Q38. Case Study 3 - Integrals\n223: A particle moves along a straight line such that its velocity at time t seconds is v(t) = 3t² - 12t + 9 (in m/s). It is given that the particle starts at the origin, i.e. s(0) = 0.\n224: (i) Find the displacement function s(t).  (2 marks)\n225: (ii) Find the displacement of the particle at t = 2 s.  (1 mark)\n226: (iii) Find the value(s) of t ∈ [0, 3] at which the particle is momentarily at rest.  (1 mark)\n227: \n228: -- End of Question Paper --\n229: \nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
