{"step_index":38,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-24T15:52:08Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-24T21:22:08+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-24T21:22:08+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/rajan/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/math_exam_2026/Set_A_Marking_Scheme.txt`\nTotal Lines: 729\nTotal Bytes: 27585\nShowing lines 1 to 60\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ================================================================================\n2: HALF-YEARLY EXAMINATION 2026-27\n3: CLASS XII - MATHEMATICS (SET A)\n4: MARKING SCHEME & STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTIONS\n5: Time Allowed: 3 Hours                                          Maximum Marks: 80\n6: ================================================================================\n7: \n8: GENERAL GUIDELINES FOR EVALUATORS:\n9: 1. The marking scheme provides general guidelines to reduce subjectivity in the marking. The answers given in the marking scheme are suggested answers.\n10: 2. Marks should be awarded for every correct step/stage as indicated in the marking scheme.\n11: 3. If a student has attempted an alternative method and arrived at the correct answer with proper steps, full credit should be awarded.\n12: 4. For MCQs, 1 mark is awarded for the correct option along with correct reasoning/justification.\n13: \n14: ================================================================================\n15: SECTION A (20 Marks - 1 Mark Each)\n16: ================================================================================\n17: \n18: Q1. \n19: Solution:\n20: Given A = {1, 2, 3} and R = {(1, 1), (2, 2), (3, 3), (1, 2)}.\n21: - Reflexive: Since (1, 1), (2, 2), (3, 3) ∈ R, (a, a) ∈ R for all a ∈ A. Hence R is reflexive.\n22: - Symmetric: (1, 2) ∈ R, but (2, 1) ∉ R. Hence R is not symmetric.\n23: - Transitive: The only pair of relations to test is (1, 1) ∈ R and (1, 2) ∈ R ⇒ (1, 2) ∈ R; and (1, 2) ∈ R and (2, 2) ∈ R ⇒ (1, 2) ∈ R. Thus, R is transitive.\n24: Therefore, R is reflexive and transitive only.\n25: Correct Option: (b) Reflexive and transitive only                         [1 mark]\n26: \n27: --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n28: Q2. \n29: Solution:\n30: Given f : ℝ → ℝ defined by f(x) = x².\n31: - Injective (One-one): f(1) = 1² = 1 and f(-1) = (-1)² = 1. Since f(1) = f(-1) but 1 ≠ -1, f is not one-one.\n32: - Surjective (Onto): For any negative real number y < 0 (e.g., y = -4 ∈ ℝ), there is no x ∈ ℝ such that x² = -4. Thus, Range(f) = [0, ∞) ≠ ℝ (Codomain). Hence f is not onto.\n33: Therefore, f is neither one-one nor onto.\n34: Correct Option: (d) Neither one-one nor onto                             [1 mark]\n35: \n36: --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n37: Q3. \n38: Solution:\n39: Principal value branch of sin⁻¹x is [-π/2, π/2].\n40: Let θ = sin⁻¹(-1/2).\n41: ⇒ sin θ = -1/2 = -sin(π/6) = sin(-π/6).\n42: Since -π/6 ∈ [-π/2, π/2], the principal value is -π/6.\n43: Correct Option: (b) -π/6                                                 [1 mark]\n44: \n45: --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n46: Q4. \n47: Solution:\n48: The domain of cos⁻¹(u) is given by -1 ≤ u ≤ 1.\n49: Here, u = 2x - 1.\n50: ⇒ -1 ≤ 2x - 1 ≤ 1\n51: Adding 1 to all sides:\n52: ⇒ 0 ≤ 2x ≤ 2\n53: Dividing by 2:\n54: ⇒ 0 ≤ x ≤ 1\n55: Therefore, the domain is [0, 1].\n56: Correct Option: (a) [0, 1]                                               [1 mark]\n57: \n58: --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n59: Q5. \n60: Solution:\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
